\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value from right to left, borrowing where needed.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{ccccccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 4\\end{array}} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 14\\end{array}} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{5} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{4} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} - \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 4 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,456,546 - 1,224,292 = 232,254\n$$"] [17592186192427 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/04/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{2}\\right)]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192428 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan{(4x)},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/9a/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192429 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0\n$$\n\n![](images/19/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192430 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1p e^{px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}\\,]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{-1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^{-3x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^{-3x} = \\infty\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{-3x} =0\n$$\n\n![](images/bc/bc4ffc41105491f2bd6b37b2482afc64946d8f67f6db83dfa322c400ae4bc2b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3a}-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (0-{e}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192439 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the perimeter of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Perimeter} = 2 \\times \\textrm{Length} + 2 \\times \\textrm{Width}\n$$\n\nIn our example, the perimeter is $42 \\, \\textrm{in}$ and the length is $12 \\, \\textrm{in}$. Let's denote the unknown width as $x$.\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n42 = 2 \\times 12 + 2 \\times x\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2 \\times 12} = {\\color{red}24}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n42 = 24 + 2 \\cdot x\n$$\n\nNow, to find $x$, we try each of the given values, starting from the smallest:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24+2\\times 7 & =38\\ne 42\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 24+2\\times 8 & =40\\ne 42\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 24+2\\times {\\color{blue}{9}} & =42\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the laptop screen's width is $9 \\, \\textrm{in}$."] [17592186192440 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the perimeter of a rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Perimeter} = 2 \\times \\textrm{Length} + 2 \\times \\textrm{Width}\n$$\n\nIn our example, the perimeter is $18 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ and the width is $3 \\, \\textrm{cm}$. Let's denote the unknown length as $x$.\n\n![](images/a9/a9c70604536943f83bc83f7b987bf149b38487045376de52b693615a6c51480d.png)\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n18 = 2 \\times x+2 \\times 3\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2 \\times 3} = {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n18 = 2 \\cdot x + 6\n$$\n\nNow, to find $x$, we try each of the given values, starting from the smallest:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times 4+6 & =14\\ne 18\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 2\\times 5+6 & =16\\ne 18\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{red}{\\times }} \\\\ 2\\times {\\color{blue}{6}}+6 & =18\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangle's length is $6 \\, \\textrm{cm}$.\n\n**Note**: The symbol\n\n$$\n\\neq\n$$\n\nmeans \"is not equal to.\""] [17592186192455 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (0-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =-4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =-4\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192456 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u = x^2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/46/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192465 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int b^{\\,px + q}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac1{p\\ln(b)} b^{\\,px+q} + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{2x+1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{1}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{2x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{0}-{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=2^{2x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} 2^{2x} = 0\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} 2^{2x} = \\infty\n$$\n\n![](images/00/009f467677932d0ac17e1632c31f4d3aa5271ffe332730d9670e69862b243bef.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3\\cdot {2}^{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{2a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192466 :question/worked-solution "The $x$-coordinate of $A$ corresponds to a solution of the equation\n\n$$\n-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -2x+6\n$$\n\nThe absolute value graph\n\n$$\ny=-\\mid x-2 \\mid\n$$\n\nhas two branches:\n\n- where $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, which has a negative slope, and\n- where $-\\mid x-2|=x-2$, which has a positive slope.\n\nNow, let's consider each branch in turn:\n\n- No points of intersection lie on the branch with a positive slope, so we can ignore this branch.\n- The point $A$ lies on the branch with a negative slope. So, if $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle - (x - 2)|=-2x + 6; - x + 2|=-2x + 6; x + 2|= 6; x|= 4.\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-|x-2| & =-2x+6 \\\\ -|4-2| & =-2(4)+6 \\\\ -|2| & =-8+6 \\\\ -|2| & =-2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $x=4$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $4$."] [17592186192604 :question/worked-solution "To construct a confidence interval, we start with a random variable that depends on the samples and has a known distribution under certain conditions.\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $$\n\\displaystyle Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/\\left(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)),\n$$\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n\nIt is known that if the samples are paired and the differences between each pair are normally distributed with a population mean $\\mu$ and known population variance, $Z$ has a standard normal distribution.\n\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n\nWe wish to find an interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/ce/ce18d66a53578e13b52bc7d6b6fd44ab6320ed98795be5621ae892727e7ef8eb.png)\n\nSince we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval must be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 - 0.95}{2} = 0.025\n$$\n\nWe can find the endpoints of such interval using a table of values for the normal distribution.\n\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{aligned}-{z}_{0.025} & <Z<{z}_{0.025} \\\\ -{z}_{0.025} & <\\frac{(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-\\mu }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}<{z}_{0.025}\\end{aligned}$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nto get rid of the denominator:\n\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply all sides by $-1$:\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}$:\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite our inequality so that the smaller terms are on the left-hand side.\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we write our result more compactly.\n\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $$\n\\displaystyle Z|= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/\\left(\\frac{σ}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)); = ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - μ)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)),\n$$\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{aligned}-{z}_{0.025} & <Z<{z}_{0.025} \\\\ -{z}_{0.025} & <\\frac{(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-\\mu }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}<{z}_{0.025}\\end{aligned}$\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$"] [17592186192938 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2t)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/46/4612e37c6c611cbaa44c09f4dcc47d006ad45403fff9af5b4df7dca71693dd6e.png)\n\n \n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192939 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/b9/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193113 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {b}^{\\,px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)}{b}^{\\,px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{2\\cdot {5}^{x/2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x/2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x/2}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x/2}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x/2}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/14/141d1745dc939ca63155ca3313257f385af6d3c505c16bd63ba3b91e1e0ba64b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193114 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={t}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=3{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/c8/c870dd367332455f2d4c35f85a188c64385a5195ca1058405a93f84c4ce31fd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193232 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={e}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f55b37b456a6ab60c495c64be5f7afe510dc0889890ed2a070b371f9bbe69c1a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193237 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-\\sqrt{2}u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=\\infty $\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}x}}=0$\n\n![](images/5e/5e9932629e5a201c2ada61157fdfa39351962a55116e2980157b1823907159f2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-\\sqrt{2x}}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193390 :question/problem "Compute $P(X\\ge 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}8{e}^{-8x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186193392 :question/problem "Compute $P(X>8)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<3.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186193802 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to conduct hypothesis tests for the difference between the means of two normal populations with *unknown* and *unequal* population variances.\n\nSuppose we have two normal populations $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$X \\sim N(\\mu_x, \\sigma^2_x), \\qquad Y \\sim N(\\mu_y, \\sigma^2_y)$\nwhere $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means, and $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are the population variances.\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the difference between the sample means is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x - \\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means, and $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes. By $z$-scoring this result, we can express this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nIn most practical situations, the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are unknown and must be replaced with estimates $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$. In this situation, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= ((X^(―) - Y^(―)) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{S_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfollows a $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe can approximate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ in various ways.\n\n- *Method 1:* We can approximate the degrees of freedom using the so-called **Welch-Satterthwaite approximation:**\n$$\n\\displaystyle k \\approx \\frac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_x^2}{n_x} \\right)^2}{n_x - 1} + \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_y^2}{n_y} \\right)^2}{n_y - 1}}\n$$\nwhere the final result is rounded *down* to the nearest integer.\n- *Method 2:* We can also approximate the degrees of freedom using the formula\n$k = \\min(n_x - 1, n_y - 1)$\nwhere $\\min(a,b)$ denotes the **minimum** of $a$ and $b$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll use the second method. You'll get practice applying the first method in separate lessons.\n\nPlease bear in mind that this method is simpler but also more conservative:\n\n- Under Method 2, our estimate for the degrees of freedom will be *smaller* than Method 1.\n- Thus, the tails of the resulting distribution will be fatter, leading to *larger* critical values for a given significance level $\\alpha$. Note that \"larger critical values\" here means larger in the absolute sense, i.e.,\n$$\n|t_{k_1}| >  |t_{k_2}|\n$$\nwhenever $k_1 < k_2$ for critical values $t_{k_1}$ and $t_{k_2}$.\n- Therefore, the probability of a Type I error (i.e., rejecting $H_0$ when it is true) *decreases.*\n\nRemember that as $k\\to\\infty$, the $t$-distribution approaches $N(0,1)$. The standard normal curve has *extremely thin* tails!\n\n![](images/5c/5c73c5fd8613e99814ab47a1a41a46a4511991d3c7eeca33eefe5913a907c10c.png)"] [17592186193838 :tutorial/content "A common type of improper integral is one where the integrand (i.e., the function being integrated) is an exponential function. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{1} e^{x} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral because the lower limit is unbounded. It can be interpreted as the shaded area under the curve $y=e^{x}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa4ff7646948570d3aa3fe6668977b853c5157f256f04d60f1768b63f11cf09d.png)\n\nWe proceed as we would with any other improper integral. We set the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrate, and take the limit as $a \\to -\\infty{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{1}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(e-{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=e^x,$ shown in the graph above:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} e^x = 0\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^x = \\infty\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can solve our integral by evaluating the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(e-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =e-0 \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193852 :tutorial/content "An important class of improper integrals concerns those involving arctangent.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following improper integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}  \\, \\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C.\n$$\n\nSo, to evaluate our improper integral, we follow the usual procedure:\n\n- First, we set the upper bound equal to some parameter $a.$\n\n- Then, we integrate as usual.\n\n- Finally, we take the limit as $a \\to \\infty.$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\big[ \\arctan(x) \\big] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\big[ \\arctan(x) \\big] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/27/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x  = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186193855 :tutorial/content "We wish to find the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\vert x + 1 \\vert = 2x - 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nTherefore, we have the following two cases:\n\n$$\n|x+1| = x+1 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad |x+1| = -(x+1)\n$$\n\nWe now consider these two cases separately:\n\n- If $\\mid x + 1|=x + 1$, we obtain the following equation:\n$x+1 = 2x-4$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x + 1|= 2x - 4; 1|= x - 4; 5|= x; x|= 5\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid x + 1 \\mid |= 2x - 4; \\mid 5 + 1 \\mid |= 2(5) - 4; \\mid 6 \\mid |= 10 - 4; 6|= 6✓\n$$\nSince $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid x + 1|=-(x + 1)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid x + 1 \\mid |= 2x - 4; - (x + 1)|= 2x - 4; - x - 1|= 2x - 4; - 1|= 3x - 4; 3|= 3x; 1|= x; x|= 1.\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid x + 1 \\mid |= 2x - 4; \\mid 1 + 1 \\mid |= 2(1) - 4; \\mid 2 \\mid |= 2 - 4; 2|=-2\\cdot\n$$\nSince $x=1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution. It's called an **extraneous** solution.\n\nTherefore, the *only* solution of our equation is $x = 5$. This solution corresponds to the point $P$, shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9bed877b267808bd54f0cdc43db51264f9e94b41345da67a59fed461d77d4504.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Whenever we want to solve an absolute value equation where the variable is on both sides, we must *always* check that the solutions satisfy the original equation.\n\nSome absolute value equations have no solutions at all! Let's see an example."] [17592186193896 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **critical region** contains all $x$-values that are *collectively* unlikely under the null hypothesis and therefore cause the null hypothesis to be rejected.\n\nFor two-tailed tests, the critical region typically consists of *two* disjoint sets. Consequently, there are, typically, *two* critical values.\n\nThe critical region for the hypothesis test we just carried out is shown below.\n\n![](images/72/7234fd53c36f99dae631885c2693467daae4f823d1dab2b23ead7a6dbe7a9da7.png)\n\nAs we saw, $X=2$ does not lie in the critical region. Let's consider the possible value of $X$ that *do* lie in the critical region:\n\n- For the left tail, it's easy to show that the values $X=0$ and $X=1$ lie in the critical region, but $X=2$ does not.\n$\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0) & =0.1\\mathrm{\\%}<2.5\\mathrm{\\%}\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\le 1) & =1.07\\mathrm{\\%}<2.5\\mathrm{\\%}\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =5.47\\mathrm{\\%}≮2.5\\mathrm{\\%}\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}$\n- For the right tail, $X=10$ and $X=9$ lie in the critical region, but $X=8$ does not.\n$\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 10) & =0.1\\mathrm{\\%}<2.5\\mathrm{\\%}\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\ge 9) & =1.07\\mathrm{\\%}<2.5\\mathrm{\\%}\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\ge 8) & =5.47\\mathrm{\\%}≮2.5\\mathrm{\\%}\\hspace{2em} & {\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The critical region for this hypothesis test is\n$X\\leq 1\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad X\\geq 9$.\n- The critical values are $X=1$ and $X=9$."] [17592186198143 :question/worked-solution "A *null sequence* is a sequence that converges to zero:\n\n- Sequence I is a null sequence. By dividing the numerator and denominator by $n^2$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(n → ∞)a_{n}|= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n}{n^{2} + 1}\\right); = \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}((\\left(\\frac{n}{n^{2}}\\right))/((\\frac{n^{2}}{n^{2}} + \\frac{1}{n^{2}}))); = \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}((\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right))/((1 + \\frac{1}{n^{2}}))); = \\frac{0}{1 + 0}; = 0✓\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is *not* a null sequence since\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(n → ∞)b_{n}|= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\sqrt{n + 1}; = ∞; \\ne 0.\\cdot\n$$\n- Sequence III is null. Notice that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r =\\dfrac{8}{9}$.\nSince $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence converges to zero:\n$$\n\\displaystyle lim_(n → ∞)c_{n}|= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)^{n}; = 0✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186198145 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the smallest natural number $N$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left| \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n \\right| < 0.000\\,4\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n\\right| = \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the following inequality for $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n} & <0.000\\hspace{0.167em}4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n} & <\\operatorname{ln}(0.000\\hspace{0.167em}4) \\\\ n\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & <\\operatorname{ln}(0.000\\hspace{0.167em}4) \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(0.000\\hspace{0.167em}4)}{\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}=19.296...\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\left| \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n \\right| < 0.000\\,4\n$$\n\nfor all natural numbers\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\lceil19.296... \\rceil =20\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{20}}\n$$"] [17592186198146 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & <ε \\\\ 1 & <ε\\sqrt{n} \\\\ 1 & <n{ε}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{ε}^{2}} & <n \\\\ n & >\\frac{1}{{ε}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$"] [17592186198147 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nWriting this in words, we have:\n\n> We need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$.\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for $n$:\n\n> Solving this inequality for $n$, we get\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & <ε \\\\ 1 & <ε\\sqrt{n} \\\\ 1 & <n{ε}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{ε}^{2}} & <n \\\\ n & >\\frac{1}{{ε}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Therefore, we choose $N > \\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}$.\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\mid |= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}; < \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1/ε^{2}}}; = ε.\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0.\n$$"] [17592186198148 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3n-7} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn\\geq 3\n$$\n\nSo for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 3\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{2}{3n-7} \\right| = \\dfrac{2}{3n-7}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3n-7} & <ε \\\\ 2 & <3nε-7ε \\\\ 2+7ε & <3nε \\\\ \\frac{2}{3ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <n \\\\ n & >\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{2}{3ε}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac 73 + \\dfrac{2}{3\\varepsilon}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3\\varepsilon} > 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 73 + \\dfrac{2}{3\\varepsilon} > \\dfrac 73 = 2 \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN \\geq 3\n$$"] [17592186198162 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\{9-x^2=0 \\mid x \\in \\Bbb Q \\}\n$$\n\nGiven the set $A$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mid A \\mid = 2$\n2. $|A| = |\\mathbb{Z}|$\n3. $|A \\cup \\mathbb{Z}| =|\\mathbb{N}|$"] [17592186198162 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a finite set and a countably infinite set is countably infinite.\n\nAlso, note that\n\n- $A =\\{-3, 3\\}$ is finite with $\\mid A|=2$, and\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ and $\\mathbb{Z}$ are countably infinite with the same cardinality.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set $A$ is finite with $\\mid A|=2$.\n- Statement II is false. The set $A$ is finite with $\\mid A|=2$. On the other hand, $\\mathbb{Z}$ is infinite with $\\mid \\mathbb{Z}|=\\aleph_0$. So,\n$$\n| A | =2 \\neq \\aleph_0 = |\\mathbb{Z}|.\n$$\n- Statement III is true. The set $A$ is finite, while $\\mid \\mathbb{Z} \\mid = \\aleph_0$. As a result, $\\mid A \\cup \\mathbb{Z} \\mid = \\aleph_0$. On the other hand, we know that $\\mid \\mathbb{N} \\mid = \\aleph_0$. So,\n$$\n|A \\cup \\mathbb{Z}| = \\aleph_0 = |\\mathbb{N}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186198171 :question/worked-solution "A set $A$ is *countably infinite* (or *denumerable*) if\n\n$$\n|A| = |\\mathbb{N}|.\n$$\n\nA set is *countable* if it is either finite or countably infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $A$ has infinitely many elements.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle A|= {(-2)^{n}∣n ∈ N}; = {-2, 4,-8, 16,-32…}\n$$\ncan be represented as a (one-way infinite) sequence. So, it's countably infinite (or denumerable).\n- Statement III is true since any countably infinite set is also countable.\n- Statement IV is false since statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186198173 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the smallest natural number $N$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{9}{7}\\right)^n > 8\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nSolving the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)}^{n} & >8\\hspace{0.167em}000\\hspace{0.167em}000 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}{\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)}^{n} & >\\operatorname{ln}(8\\hspace{0.167em}000\\hspace{0.167em}000) \\\\ n\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right) & >\\operatorname{ln}(8\\hspace{0.167em}000\\hspace{0.167em}000) \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(8\\hspace{0.167em}000\\hspace{0.167em}000)}{\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)}\\approx 63.247.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{9}{7}\\right)^n > 8\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nfor all natural numbers\n\n$$\nn > \\lceil 63.247 \\rceil = 64\n$$\n\nTherefore, $N = 64$."] [17592186198174 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the smallest natural number $N$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10 - 3n^2}{n} < -25\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nSolving the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-3{n}^{2}}{n} & <-25 \\\\ 10-3{n}^{2} & <-25n \\\\ 3{n}^{2}-25n-10 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we need to find the values of $n$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 3n^2 - 25n - 10\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $n$-axis.\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1, 2} &= (- (-25) ± \\sqrt{(-25)^{2} - 4(3)(-10)})/(2(3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{25 ± \\sqrt{745}}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are\n\n$$\nn_1\\approx -0.382\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn_2\\approx 8.716\n$$\n\nand we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/2a/2a102c601f681678aed75f944d50a2bcb4c6ad36b95649a3e583a14ac271ca9e.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $n$-values where the parabola lies above the axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality (to three decimal places) is\n$n < -0.382\\quad \\text{or}\\quad n > 8.716$.\n\nAs a result, the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10 - 3n^2}{n} < -25\n$$\n\nis satisfied for all natural numbers\n\n$$\nn > \\lceil 8.716\\ldots \\rceil = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nN = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}\n$$"] [17592186198176 :question/worked-solution "First, we solve the inequality $a_n < M$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-3n & <M \\\\ 1-M & <3n \\\\ \\frac{1-M}{3} & <n \\\\ n & >\\frac{1-M}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{1-M}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1-3n < M$ for all natural numbers\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$"] [17592186198177 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $2\\sqrt[3]{n}- 3 > 0$ for any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 4\n$$\n\nSo, we solve the inequality $a_n > M$ for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 4{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{n}-3 & >M \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{n} & >M+3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{n} & >\\frac{M+3}{2} \\\\ n & >\\frac{(M+3)^{3}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{(M+3)^3}{8}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since $M > 0$, we have that\n\n$$\n((M + 3)^{3})/(8) \\mid > \\frac{3^{3}}{8}; > \\frac{27}{8}; > 3\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN \\geq 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\sqrt[3]{n}- 3 > M$ for all natural numbers\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$"] [17592186198178 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (4-n^2) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall M < 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: (4-n^2) \\lt M\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\n> We need to show that for any real $M < 0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $4-n^2 < M$.\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $M$.\n\nFirst, notice that $4-n^2 < 0$ for any\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3\n$$\n\nSo, we solve the inequality $4-n^2 < M$ for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-{n}^{2} & <M \\\\ -{n}^{2} & <M-4 \\\\ {n}^{2} & >4-M \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{4-M}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $4-n^2 < M$, it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{4-M}$.\n\nNotice that, since $M < 0$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4 - M} > \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN\\geq 3\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $M < 0$. Let's choose some natural number\n> $N > \\sqrt{4-M}$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $a_{n}|= 4 - n^{2}; < 4 - (\\sqrt{4 - M})^{2}; < 4 - 4 + M; = M$.\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (4-n^2) = -\\infty.\n$$"] [17592186198179 :question/worked-solution "Recall that sets $A$ and $B$ have the same cardinality if there exists a bijection (i.e., a function that's injective and surjective) that maps $A$ onto $B$. Also, we know that\n\n$$\n\\big\\vert \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}_0) \\big\\vert = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: \\mathcal P(\\mathbb N_0)\\to \\{-2, 2 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}_0}$ that, given $A\\subseteq \\mathbb{N}_0$, maps $A$ to the sequence $(s_0, s_1, s_2, s_3, \\ldots) \\in \\{-2, 2 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}_0}$, where\n$s_n = \n\\begin{cases}2, & \\text{if}\\hspace{0.222em}n{\\color{blue}{\\in A}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{-2}}, & \\text{if}\\hspace{0.222em}n{\\color{blue}{\\notin A}}\\end{cases}.$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{0, 1, 2 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf(A) = (s_0, s_1, s_2, s_3, \\ldots) = (2, 2, 2, -2, \\ldots)\n$$\n\nwhere all terms after $s_3$ are equal to $-2$.\n\nThis function $f$ is both\n\n- injective, since $f(A_1) \\neq f(A_2)$ wherever $A_1 \\neq A_2$, and\n- surjective, since each sequence in $\\{-2, 2 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}_0}$ has a preimage in $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}_0)$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ is a bijection from $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}_0)}$ onto $\\{-2, 2 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}_0}$.\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\n\\big| \\{ -2, 2  \\}^{\\mathbb{N}_0} \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}_0) \\big| = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathfrak{c}}.\n$$"] [17592186198181 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the power set of a countably infinite set possesses the cardinality of the continuum.\n\nFirst, let's solve the restricting inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3x-2| & <1 \\\\ -1 & <3x-2\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & <3x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & <x<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $A$ consists of all rational numbers in the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac13,1\\right)\n$$\n\nThis set is infinite, and since it's an infinite subset of the countably infinite set $\\mathbb{Q}$, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n|A| = |\\mathbb{N}| = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\aleph_0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n|\\mathcal{P}(A)| = |\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})| = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathfrak{c}}.\n$$"] [17592186198189 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the smallest natural number $N$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{5^n+1}{5^n+6}- 1 \\right| < 0.001\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nFirst, simplifying $\\mid a_n - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{{5}^{n}+1}{{5}^{n}+6}-1| \\\\  & =|\\frac{{5}^{n}+1}{{5}^{n}+6}-\\frac{{5}^{n}+6}{{5}^{n}+6}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{{5}^{n}+1-{5}^{n}-6}{{5}^{n}+6}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-5}{{5}^{n}+6}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{5}{{5}^{n}+6}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{5^n+6} > 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n - L \\mid = \\left|- \\dfrac{5}{5^n+6} \\right| = \\dfrac{5}{5^n+6}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the following inequality for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{5}^{n}+6} & <0.001 \\\\ 5 & <0.001({5}^{n}+6) \\\\ \\frac{5}{0.001} & <{5}^{n}+6 \\\\ \\frac{5}{0.001}-6 & <{5}^{n} \\\\ 4\\hspace{0.167em}994 & <{5}^{n} \\\\ {log}_{5}(4\\hspace{0.167em}994) & <n \\\\ n & >{log}_{5}(4\\hspace{0.167em}994)\\approx 5.291\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{5^n+1}{5^n+6}- 1 \\right| < 0.001\n$$\n\nfor all natural numbers\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\lceil 5.291 \\rceil = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $N = 6$."] [17592186198190 :question/worked-solution "First, simplifying $\\mid a_n - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{9{n}^{2}+1}{3{n}^{2}-2}-3| \\\\  & =|\\frac{9{n}^{2}+1}{3{n}^{2}-2}-\\frac{3(3{n}^{2}-2)}{3{n}^{2}-2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{9{n}^{2}+1-9{n}^{2}+6}{3{n}^{2}-2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{7}{3{n}^{2}-2}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $a_n - L \\gt 0$ for any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\left| a_n - L \\right| = \\left|\\dfrac{7}{3n^2-2}\\right| = \\dfrac{7}{3n^2-2}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n - L \\mid \\lt \\varepsilon$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3{n}^{2}-2} & <ε \\\\ 7 & <ε(3{n}^{2}-2) \\\\ \\frac{7}{ε} & <3{n}^{2}-2 \\\\ \\frac{7}{ε}+2 & <3{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3ε}+\\frac{2}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ {n}^{2} & >\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{7}{3ε} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{7}{3ε}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN \\gt \\sqrt{\\dfrac23+\\dfrac{7}{3\\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{9n^2+1}{3n^2-2} - 3 \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nfor all natural numbers\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$"] [17592186198191 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon \\gt 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, we simplifying $\\mid a_n - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}-(-1)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+1| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+\\frac{{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}+{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n - L \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\right| = \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n - L \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10} & <ε \\\\ 9 & <ε({4}^{n}-10) \\\\ \\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n}-10 \\\\ 10+\\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n} \\\\ {log}_{4}(10+\\frac{9}{ε}) & <n \\\\ n & >{log}_{4}(10+\\frac{9}{ε}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} - (-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n\nNotice that, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon} > 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right) > \\log_4(10)\\approx 1.66\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1) \\mid |= \\frac{9}{4^{n} - 10}; < (9)/(4^{\\log_{4} (10 + 9/ε)} - 10); = (9)/((10 + \\frac{9}{ε} - 10)); = ε.\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition,\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1) \\mid \\mid < (9)/(4^{\\log_{4} (10 + 9/ε)} - 10); = (9)/((10 + \\frac{9}{ε} - 10)); = ε.\n$$\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} = -1.\n$$"] [17592186198209 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5)\n$$\n\nWe start by checking that the statement makes sense in the given domain of $n{:}$\n\n> Notice that the expression $4^{2n-3}+ 5$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 2$.\n\nRecall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $3$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $3{:}$\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 4^{2n-3}+ 5 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nNow, we proceed as follows:\n\n> Working in modulo $3$, we have the following:\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2n-3}+5 & ≡(1)^{2n-3}+2 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3) \\\\  & ≡1+2 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3) \\\\  & ≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $3 \\mid (4^{2n-3}+ 5)$, as required."] [17592186198210 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $3$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $3{:}$\n\n> According to the definition of modular congruence,\n> $3 \\mid (n^3-n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n^3-n \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n\nAn integer can have only $0$, $1$, or $2$ as a remainder when divided by $3$. So, we'll prove our congruence using cases.\n\n> Now, we have the following cases for $n{:}$\n\nFirst, we take $n \\equiv 0$ in our expression:\n\n> **Case 1.** Suppose $n \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$. Then:\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-n & ≡(0)^{3}-(0) & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3)\\end{aligned}$\n\nNext, we take $n \\equiv 1{:}$\n\n> **Case 2.** Suppose $n \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$. Then:\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-n & ≡(1)^{3}-(1) & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3)\\end{aligned}$\n\nThen, we take $n \\equiv 2{:}$\n\n> **Case 3.** Suppose $n \\equiv 2 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$. Then:\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}-n & ≡(2)^{3}-(2) & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3) \\\\  & ≡6 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}3)\\end{aligned}$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> In all these cases, we obtain\n> $n^3-n \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:3)$.\n> Therefore, $n^3-n$ is divisible by $3$, as required."] [17592186198214 :question/worked-solution "First, we round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}7.6 & - & 2.18 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{7.5}} & - & {\\color{blue}{2.0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.6 - 2.18$ is approximately $5.5$."] [17592186198215 :question/worked-solution 