every natural number $n \\ge N$, we have\n$\\mid \\frac{1}{2n^{2}} \\mid < ε$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$<$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}."] [17592186198819 :question/problem "The notation\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(3n + 1) = ∞$\nmeans the following:\n\nFor any real\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnatural number $N$ such that\n{{field-3}}\nnatural number\n{{field-4}} ,\nwe have\n$3n + 1$ {{field-5}}."] [17592186198820 :question/problem "The notation\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{1 - 2n^{2}}{n}\\right) = - ∞$\nmeans the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\nreal $M < 0$\nthere exists a\n{{field-2}}\nnumber $N$ such that for every\n{{field-3}}\nnumber $n$, if\n{{field-4}} ,\nthen $<$ {{field-5}} {{field-6}}"] [17592186198822 :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{1}{3n - 4} = 0$.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\ge N$, we have\n$\\mid \\frac{1}{3n - 4} \\mid < ε$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$>$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}."] [17592186198826 :question/problem "Using the formal definition, prove that\n\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\sqrt{n + 1} = ∞$.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $M > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\ge N$, we have\n$\\sqrt{n + 1} > M$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$>$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}."] [17592186198827 :question/problem "Using the formal definition, prove that\n\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(2 - 3n) = - ∞$.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $M < 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\ge N$, we have\n$2 - 3n < M$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$>$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}."] [17592186198846 :question/problem "The notation $\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{3n}{n + 3}\\right) = 3$ means the following:\n\nFor any real\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnatural number $N$ such that\n{{field-3}}\nnatural numbers\n{{field-4}},\nwe have\n{{field-5}} {{field-6}}."] [17592186198850 :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{n}{n + 1} = 1$.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\ge N$, we have\n\n$\\mid \\frac{n}{n + 1} - 1 \\mid < ε$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$<$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}."] [17592186198856 :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\frac{3^{n + 1} + 1}{3^{n} - 2} = 3$.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\ge N$, we have\n\n$\\mid \\frac{3^{n + 1} + 1}{3^{n} - 2} - 3 \\mid < ε$.\n\nSolving this inequality for $n$, we get\n$>$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nTherefore, we choose $N$\n{{field-3}} {{field-4}}."] [17592186199482 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate the triple integral of a function $f(x,y,z)$ over a rectangular domain $R,$ given by \n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times[c,d]\\times[e,f].\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate triple integrals over type I regions in three-dimensional space.\n\nBefore we begin, we need to discuss the meaning behind the triple integral of a function over a non-rectangular domain. To help us, consider the region $R$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ba/ba2c52d81578e4754732a99f459b037bb7ceb35b60e3a711ed239855d837a93f.png)\n\nLet's discuss how to define the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 4xyz\\,\\textrm d V\n$$\n\nWe start by enclosing the region $R$ by a rectangular solid $R'$ whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e5548c26288c4b8ba6ac9e450ad0a78be55ae6a6ab6e4ef7a52a086bc997ed11.png)\n\nNow, we extend the integrand to the rectangular region $R'$ by defining a new function $f(x,y,z)$ that's equal to $0$ outside $R$ and equals $4xyz$ inside $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}4xyz, & (x,y,z)\\in {R}^{'}\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y,z)\\in {R}^{'}∖R\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nAlthough $f(x,y,z)$ might not be continuous at the boundary of $R,$ the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the rectangular domain $R'$ will exist. \n\nAs usual, it's the unique number $I$ that satisfies the inequality\n\\[\nL_f(P) \\leq I \\leq U_f(P)\n\\]\nfor all partitions $P$ of the rectangle $R.$ \n\nTherefore, our original triple integral is defined as \n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} 4xyz \\, \\textrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{R'} f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}V.\n$$"] [17592186199548 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $a_n$ is a sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe know that the *limit* of a sequence (if it exists) is the unique number $L$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = L\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this statement means that the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want, provided that $n$ is large enough.\n\nWhile intuition is important, none of this is very precise. What does \"the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want\" mean? And what about \"provided that $n$ is large enough\"? Can we quantify these ideas more precisely?\n\nWe need a precise mathematical definition of what's meant by *the limit of a sequence.* This lesson aims to construct a suitable definition in the case where $L=0$.\n\nWe start with a definition.\n\n> *A **null sequence** is a sequence that converges to zero.*\n\nFor example, the sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{1+n^2}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}a_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{1}{1 + n^{2}}\\right) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the sequence\n\n$$\nb_n = n+1, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis *not* a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}b_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(n + 1) \\\\\n&= ∞ \\\\\n&\\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186199554 :tutorial/content "A set $A$ is called **countably infinite** (or **denumerable**) if it has the same cardinality as the set of natural numbers.\n\n$$\n|A| = |\\mathbb{N}|\n$$\n\nIn other words, $A$ is countably infinite if there exists a bijection between $A$ and the set of natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$. Elements of a countably infinite set can be listed as a *sequence* indexed by natural numbers:\n\n$$\na_1, \\: a_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: a_n, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nA set is **countable** if it is either finite or countably infinite.\n\nThe cardinality of the natural numbers (i.e., all countably infinite sets) is denoted by $\\aleph_0$, which reads as \"aleph-zero\" or \"aleph-null.\"\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mathbb{N} \\mid = \\aleph_0\n$$\n\nAleph-null is the smallest infinite cardinality in the following sense:\n\n> *Any subset of a countably infinite set is either finite or countably infinite.*\n\nConsequently, any infinite subset of a countably infinite set is countably infinite.\n\nFinally, a set is called **uncountable** if it's infinite but not countable (i.e., there is no bijection between $\\mathbb{N}$ and this set). We will see examples of uncountable sets in future lessons."] [17592186199563 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = 2\\sqrt{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nNote that this sequence is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(2\\sqrt n\\right) = \\infty\n$$\n\nGiven that $M > 0$, let's find a natural number $N(M)$ such that for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na_n > M\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality $a_n > M$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{n} & >M \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{M}{2} \\\\ n & >{\\left(\\frac{M}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\frac{{M}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Given any $M >0$, no matter how large, $a_n$ is guaranteed to be larger than $M$ provided that\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the smallest natural number larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$"] [17592186199566 :tutorial/content "Recall that the power set of a finite $X$, denoted $\\mathcal{P}(X)$, contains every subset of $S$, including $X$ itself and the empty set.\n\nWe've already seen that if $X$ is *finite*, the cardinality of $\\mathcal{P}(X)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\big|\\mathcal{P}(X)\\big| = 2^{|X|}.\n$$\n\nWe know from previous lessons that the cardinality of the set of natural numbers is denoted $\\aleph_0$, so\n\n$$\n\\big \\vert \\mathbb N\\big \\vert = \\aleph_0\n$$\n\nThe power set of $\\mathbb N$, denoted $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)$, is an infinite set that contains every subset of the natural numbers. Taking inspiration from the above result, we will define the cardinality of the power set of the natural numbers as follows:\n\n$$\n\\big \\vert \\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)\\big \\vert = 2^{\\aleph_0}\n$$\n\nLoosely speaking, $\\mathcal P (\\mathbb N)$ is \"larger\" than $\\mathbb N$. Can we quantify this more precisely? Indeed, we can. In fact, the cardinality of $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb{N})$ is deeply connected to that of $(0,1)$, as given by the following result:\n\n> *The power set of $\\mathbb{N}$ has the same cardinality as the interval $(0,1)$, i.e.,*\n> $\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = 2^{\\aleph_0} = \\big| (0,1) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.$.\n\nThis means that there is a bijection that maps every real number $a$ from $(0,1)$ to a subset $A$ of natural numbers. We'll prove this result later in the lesson.\n\n![](images/33/33fc45904c0eb6ef0da7cfd0947577ca5f2885c9bb1f1a79ff80b16c8ee9b5ad.png)\n\nMore generally, for *any* countably infinite set $X$, we have\n\n$$\n\\big \\vert \\mathcal{P}(X) \\big \\vert = \\mathfrak{c}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N) \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb Z) \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb Q) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.\n$$"] [17592186199570 :tutorial/content "So, is the set of all infinite binary sequences countable?\n\nTo answer this, consider the set $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$ of all infinite binary sequences over $\\{0,1 \\}$. Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *The set of all infinite binary sequences has the cardinality of the continuum:* $\\big| \\{ 0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}} \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}$\n\nThis result implies that the set of all infinite binary sequences is *uncountable!*\n\nTo prove this, consider the function\n\n$$\nf: \\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)\\to \\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}\n$$\n\nthat, given $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$, maps $A$ to the sequence $(s_1, s_2, s_3, \\ldots) \\in \\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, where\n\n$$\ns_n = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\in  A \\\\ 0 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\notin  A .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, 4, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf(A) = (0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0, \\ldots)\n$$\n\nwhere all terms after the $5$th equal zero.\n\nThe function $f$ is a bijection from $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N})$ onto $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$. To show this, we need to show that $f$ is injective and surjective:\n\n- First, we prove $f$ is injective. Assume that the subsets $A\\subseteq \\mathbb N$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb N$ are distinct. Then, one of them should contain an element that the other doesn't contain. Without loss of generality, assume that $s \\in A$ while $s \\notin B$ for some natural number $s$. Then, the sequence $f(A)$ has $1$ as its $s$th term whereas $f(B)$ has $0$. In other words, distinct subsets give distinct sequences. So, $f$ is injective.\n- On the other hand, $f$ is surjective since for any sequence from $\\{0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, we can reconstruct the corresponding subset in $\\mathbb{N}$. For example, the sequence $(0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, \\ldots)$, where all terms after the $4$th equal zero, corresponds to $\\{2,3,4\\}$. In other words, each sequence in $\\{0,1\\}^\\mathbb{N}$ is paired with an element of $\\mathcal P (\\mathbb N)$. So, $f$ is surjective.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\big| \\{ 0,1 \\}^{\\mathbb{N}} \\big| = \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.\n$$"] [17592186199574 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the theorem stated in the introduction:\n\n> *The power set of $\\mathbb{N}$ has the same cardinality as the interval $(0,1)$, i.e., $\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = |(0,1)| = \\mathfrak{c}.$.*\n\nFirst, consider the function $f: \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N) \\to (0,1)$ that, given $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$, maps it to the decimal number\n\n$$\n0.a_1 a_2 a_3 \\ldots \\in (0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na_n = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\in  A , \\\\ 2 , & \\text{if} \\hspace{0.222em} n \\notin  A .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, 4, 5 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf(A) = 0.21211\\overline{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $f$ is injective since\n\n$$\nf(A) \\neq f(B)\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\nA \\neq B\n$$\n\nIndeed, if two subsets $A$ and $B$ are distinct, then one of them should contain an element that the other doesn't contain. Without loss of generality, assume that $s \\in A$ while $s \\notin B$ for some natural number $s$. Then, the sequence $f(A)$ has $1$ as its $s$th term whereas $f(B)$ has $2$. In other words, distinct subsets give distinct sequences. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| \\leq \\big| (0,1) \\big|.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $g: (0,1) \\to \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb N)$ that, given $0.a_1 a_2 a_3 \\ldots \\in (0,1)$, maps it to the set\n\n$$\n\\{10 a_1, 10^2 a_2, 10^3 a_3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** To avoid ambiguity, we use only those decimal representations that do not have repeating $9$'s at the end. For example,\n\n$$\n0.5=0.4\\overline{9}\n$$\n\nbut we consider only the representation $0.5$.\n\nThe function $g$ is injective since\n\n$$\ng(x) \\neq g(y)\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\nx \\neq y\n$$\n\nIndeed, if\n\n$$\nx=0.x_1x_2x_3\\ldots\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=0.y_1y_2y_3\\ldots\n$$\n\nthen $g(x)=g(y)$ would mean that\n\n$$\n\\{10 x_1, 10^2 x_2, 10^3 x_3, \\ldots \\} = \\{10 y_1, 10^2 y_2, 10^3 y_3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nSince the elements listed in each set must be distinct (the ascending powers of $10$ guarantee this), this equality is true if and only if $x_i=y_i$ for all $i \\in \\mathbb{N}$, i.e., $x=y$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\big| (0,1) \\big| \\leq \\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big|.\n$$\n\nFinally, according to the Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\big| \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{N}) \\big| = \\big| (0,1) \\big| = \\mathfrak{c}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** We did not find a concrete bijection between the underlying sets, but we showed that it exists, which is enough!"] [17592186199587 :tutorial/content "We can use modular congruences to prove statements about divisibility. Congruences often provide a more straightforward way of establishing the truth of a divisibility statement compared to other methods.\n\nFor example, let's prove the following proposition:\n\n> *$5^n - 1$ is divisible by $4$ for any integer $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 4 \\mid (5^n- 1)\n$$\n\nWe start by checking that the statement makes sense in the given domain of $n{:}$\n\n> *Notice that the expression $5^n -1$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nRecall that $n \\mid a$ is equivalent to $a \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)$. So, instead of proving divisibility by $4$, we can show that our expression is congruent to $0$ modulo $4{:}$\n\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $4 \\mid (5^n-1) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5^n -1 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n\nNow, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *Working in modulo $4$, we have the following:*\n> $\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n}-1 & ≡(1)^{n}-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡1-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4)\\end{aligned}$\n\nFinally, we make our conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, $4 \\mid (5^n -1)$, as required.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and full proof."] [17592186199596 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we saw how to define the limit of a null sequence (i.e., a sequence whose limit equals zero) and how to construct a formal proof that a given sequence is null.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include sequences that converge a finite (not necessarily zero) limit. If such a limit exists, it's usually denoted as $L$.\n\nConsider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n+1}{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that the limit of this sequence is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}}={\\color{red}{1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}a_{n} &= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n + 1}{n}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}(1 + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that the distance between $a_n$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nis *smaller than* $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\forall n\\geq N,\\qquad \\mid a_n - {\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nLet's draw a few sketches to illustrate this idea.\n\n- If we select $\\varepsilon = 0.5$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 3$.\n\n![](images/be/bee8ffa92b56e2e8dedc214322481b13a3ab8cd4b73d84a5c1300d10dbd43d4f.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.3$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 4$.\n\n![](images/d2/d27e06c19457ab1c89386cde995c043c6d6d55169ec8fdc32509690bf1880767.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.1$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 11$.\n\n![](images/56/564c5ddf12f0171c5d55da0d669f9f7bff2726707c72e473fa9e8eb6704bd937.png)\n\nThe key idea is this: By making $\\varepsilon$ smaller and smaller, we can *always* find an index $N$ where the distance between $a_n$ and $\\color{red}L$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$"] [17592186199597 :tutorial/content "We can use benchmarks to estimate decimal subtraction.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to estimate the following difference using benchmarks with decimal parts of $0.0$ or $0.5{:}$\n\n$$\n12.53 - 7.17\n$$\n\nWe first round both numbers to the nearest benchmark:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}12.53 & - & 7.17 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{12.5}} & - & {\\color{blue}{7.0}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}.\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12.53 - 7.17$ is approximately $5.5$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n12.53 - 7.17 \\approx 5.5\n$$"] [17592186199606 :tutorial/content "Consider the following null sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\varepsilon > 0$, let's find a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that our sequence is positive for any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} & <ε \\\\ 1 & <ε{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{ε} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{ε}} & <n \\\\ n & >\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{ε}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Given any $\\varepsilon > 0$, no matter how small, $\\mid a_n \\mid$ is guaranteed to be smaller than $\\varepsilon$ provided that\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the smallest natural number greater than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}\n$$"] [17592186199624 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *$5^n - 1$ is divisible by $4$ for any integer $n \\geq 1$.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Notice that the expression $5^n -1$ gives us an integer value when $n \\geq 1$.*\n> *According to the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $4 \\mid (5^n-1) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5^n -1 \\equiv 0 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4)$.\n> *Working in modulo $4$, we have the following:*\n> $\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n}-1 & ≡(1)^{n}-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡1-1 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4) \\\\  & ≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}4)\\end{aligned}$\n> *Therefore, $4 \\mid (5^n -1)$, as required.*"] [17592186199735 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186199736 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199751 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199752 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186199762 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199763 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186199783 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199784 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186199795 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199796 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186199802 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186199803 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199823 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186199824 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199831 :answer/value "|A | \\ne | B|"] [17592186199832 :answer/value "|A|= | B|"] [17592186203666 :question/worked-solution "Sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$.\n\nRecall that the function $y=\\ln x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $(0,\\infty)$ onto the interval\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,\\infty) = \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f0bcbc610b1f80dc592cde2f3e976d197c8f6e9de22f1586de0de73cc06843.png)\n\nHowever, we need a function that maps $(8,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb R$ instead of $(0,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$. To construct one, we shift our logarithm function by $8$ units to the right. This can be done by subtracting $8$ from the argument.\n\nSo, we consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows:\n\n$$\nf: x \\to \\ln(x-8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $f$ bijectively maps $(8,\\infty)$ onto $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\n![](images/32/3275f2495414672116b41d4e4f1df61e173b4fd3dce6034e7c85a9cc254acaf2.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|A| = |B|,\n$$\n\ni.e., the sets have the same cardinality."]}
